  {
    "episode_id": 96498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, again, let me thank you for allowing me this opportunity. I was not only pleased to serve on the Air Corps Committee, but I was one of the conferees. So that was a special honor, and we appreciate having the opportunity to sit and try to resolve some of the problems that we had initially with the bill. But the 1890 lay-in grants, of course, are our HBCUs. They're 19 of them across the country. And we have one in North Carolina. That's North Carolina A&T. That provision that you talked about actually is very discriminatory. I mean, what it did, as you said, it restricted land grant institutions and they were supposed to spend all of their money, they couldn't really carry over. The only thing they could carry over was 20%. And that was not a rule for the 1862 land grants. They could keep all of this and spend it the way they need it to. And so that has been removed. And so the land grant institutions will be dealt with the same way. And we appreciate that. And basically it ensures greater equity between the land grant. And that provision has been around for decades and it was unfair. And we have in this bill done something about it. I did sponsor the Carryover Equity Act to fix that, you know, the current law. And so now we, you know, it's outdated. And, you know, oftentimes we have things on our books that are outdated and we really need to address those. But so, thankfully, the inclusion of the Carryover Equity Act in this 2018 Farm Bill will end this fiscally irresponsible and discriminatory practice, so we're excited about that.",
